00:14:37.394 --> 00:14:39.810
I'm going to talk to you today
about Cloud Launcher, which

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.220
is our GCP marketplace.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:42.720
And one of the
things we think about

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.480
is how do we accelerate your
development through the use

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:47.276
of ISV products.

00:14:47.276 --> 00:14:49.650
And so if you're a developer,
what are some of the things

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:50.483
that you care about?

00:14:50.483 --> 00:14:53.400
Well, if you came from the
on-prem world into Cloud,

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.680
you already know
that you can spend

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.360
a lot more time coding and less
plugging in ethernet cables.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:02.160
But even though you
come into Cloud,

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.090
you're still
configuring instances.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:06.690
If you're trying to create a
production level deployment,

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.030
you're still worrying
about firewall rules,

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.470
making sure that network
can talk to each other,

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:14.082
making sure that your SSH keys
are managed well, et cetera.

00:15:14.082 --> 00:15:15.540
So these are still
challenges, even

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:16.664
though you're in the Cloud.

00:15:16.664 --> 00:15:20.400
Although, it's much better than
being in the on-prem world.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:22.019
The next is you're
often given a task.

00:15:22.019 --> 00:15:24.060
Like hey, we're trying to
build this new website,

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:25.650
let's go figure out
how to build it.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.330
And maybe your
colleague down the hall

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:28.790
is excited about a new language.

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:29.490
Like Node.

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:31.110
And you might be
a Rails developer,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:33.220
and you might want to
get started with that.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.780
So trying things out quickly
is a very important use-case.

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:40.322
And without handholding,
you can be quite lost.

00:15:40.322 --> 00:15:42.780
This is one of those challenges
that we see with developers

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.214
today.

00:15:44.214 --> 00:15:45.630
And so when you
do find a solution

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:46.860
that you're interested
it in-- maybe

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.580
that node.JS stack that you're
just starting to learn about.

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.090
While you often want
to tutorials, and you

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.256
want getting-started guides.

00:15:52.256 --> 00:15:54.580
And you want to make sure
that your journey in using

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:56.640
that software is a great one.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.280
Because, if you're like me,
I have very little patience

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:02.760
when it comes to configuring and
debugging software that I think

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:03.800
should just work.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:07.080
So you want to make sure
that experience is great.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.290
And once you've taken the
solution, you've used it,

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.570
and you like it-- you've
tweaked it the way you want--

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.930
you [? won't ?] often take that
same concept into production.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.000
And you don't want to
have to restart everything

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.440
from scratch, all over again.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:21.150
You want to be able
to do it yourself.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.830
And you want to be
able to take all

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.870
of the configuration,
investment,

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.790
and deploy it straight
into production.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:29.509
So that's great if
you're a developer.

00:16:29.509 --> 00:16:31.050
If you're an IT
administrator, you're

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.280
supporting an organization
of developers.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:35.730
If you think along
somewhat related lines,

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.010
you're thinking about
how do I standardize

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.040
the way some third party
software is deployed, right?

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:44.220
I don't want to have one team
in one part of my organization

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.160
running the same piece of
software in one pattern,

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:49.466
and another team, maybe
in a different geography,

00:16:49.466 --> 00:16:50.590
running in a different way.

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:52.680
Makes it very
difficult to understand

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.530
security, versions, et cetera.

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:58.770
And the next is how
do I reduce cost?

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:01.410
And especially in
agile IT organizations,

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.990
how do I make sure my developers
are as productive as possible?

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:07.109
So this notion of kind
of self-service IT.

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:09.660
Where developers can
go, find solutions,

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.010
provision it themselves.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:13.260
Without having to
bother the IT department

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:15.000
for every little thing.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.079
And this plays a role
in two dimensions.

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:18.839
One is on the
technical side of, how

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:20.849
do I get infrastructure
up and running?

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:23.609
But an equal challenge
for a lot of organizations

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:27.180
is a developer wants to use some
third party piece of software,

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.910
and they now have to go
through a procurement system

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:34.260
or get approval from someone,
even to do a proof of concept.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.820
And so this is
where marketplaces

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.680
like Cloud Launcher can
fill the gap quite nicely.

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.500
And so Google Cloud
Launcher is our product.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.570
I'd say the easiest
way to get started

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:50.080
with products and services
on GCP from third parties.

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:52.860
It's integrated directly
inside of our console.

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:54.160
I'll show you a demo in a bit.

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:55.980
How many of you already
used Launcher today

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.200
or have you used it?

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:00.210
That's about half of you guys.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:03.527
We have very sophisticated
solution configuration.

00:18:03.527 --> 00:18:05.610
I think it's one of the
biggest differentiators we

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.130
have in our marketplace,
compared to other marketplaces

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:09.010
out there.

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:12.504
I'll speak more in a bit about
that, especially with the demo.

00:18:12.504 --> 00:18:13.920
We have production
grade software,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.810
so we have amazing vendors.

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:19.290
You've heard some of them in
the keynotes from this week.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:21.750
And we're constantly
adding more solutions.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:25.140
And one of the nice things about
having a central place where

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.920
you can purchase all the
software is, as a GCP customer,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.330
you get a unified bill.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:33.060
So if you're purchasing
software through the launcher,

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:34.560
it's showing up on
your Google bill.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:37.517
So we facilitate that
relationship with the ISV.

00:18:37.517 --> 00:18:39.600
You don't have to work
separately with them to get

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:42.440
the software up and running.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.590
So let's talk about the
kinds of solutions we have.

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:46.630
We have hundreds and
hundreds of products today.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:49.090
These are just a sampling of
some of the major vendors.

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:51.250
You heard about SAP
coming on board.

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:55.180
I think in Dan's
keynote yesterday.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:57.310
All logos-- all companies
you probably recognize

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:59.727
are probably used today.

00:18:59.727 --> 00:19:01.560
But in addition to those
commercial vendors,

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:04.040
we also have a custom program
we have at Google, called

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.810
Google Click to Deploy.

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:12.350
And what these are is a set of
solutions packaged by Google.

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:15.710
And they typically are
popular open source stacks.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:19.340
And what we've done is we've
taken vanilla configurations,

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:22.100
and we've put them together
and pre-deploy them

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:23.870
in these packages.

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:26.480
So if you want to create
one of these instances,

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.570
instead of doing it on your own,
you can just use one of these.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:31.010
And really the
spirit behind this

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:32.930
was our insight in
that-- the moment

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:36.230
you start in cloud, one of the
first activities you're doing

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:37.730
is trying to get a
piece of software

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:40.220
that you had maybe
on-prem, up and running.

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:41.720
And so that journey,
where you might

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:44.000
be trying to configure
a Mongo database,

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.010
and you need a cluster.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:47.660
You have three or four nodes.

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.940
That might take you
several hours to configure,

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:51.440
simply because you
have to make sure

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:53.180
that SSH keys are managed well.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:56.180
Making sure that IP addresses
are transported in config files

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:57.500
across each node.

00:19:57.500 --> 00:19:59.477
Making sure you configure
the zones correctly.

00:19:59.477 --> 00:20:02.060
And then if you want to do the
same thing in a different zone,

00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:04.340
well, you have to repeat
the whole process.

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:06.965
So we find an opportunity
to simplify that.

00:20:06.965 --> 00:20:08.590
And we have a set of
these Google Click

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:09.500
to Deploy packages.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:12.200
They are some of our
more popular solutions.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.990
Since they're pretty much open
source, vanilla configurations,

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:17.180
it's really what you
would have done yourself.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:19.190
And there's just
community supported.

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:21.290
So you can read any blog
post on the internet

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.640
and apply those steps.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.460
They just work fine.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:28.040
So next I'm going to talk to
you about the kinds of solutions

00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:29.360
we have in our launcher.

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:31.050
And so there are four types.

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:34.040
The first is virtual machines
running on Compute Engine.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:38.390
So these are VM products
that run in your account.

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.020
They launch VM instances,
and you get billed for them.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:42.860
So you pay for the
infrastructure.

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:44.529
We have several
commercial solutions,

00:20:44.529 --> 00:20:46.820
where you're paying, of
course, for the infrastructure,

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:50.000
but also the license fee
from the commercial vendor.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.560
But again, that relationship
is done through Google.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.370
So you're not having to go out
of band and purchase a software

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.760
and come back and
try to deploy it.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:59.570
These, of course,
run in your account.

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:03.410
And supports single and
multi VM's, and I'll give you

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.570
a demo of that in a bit.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.510
Well sometimes, you have
an existing relationship

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:09.240
with the vendor.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.820
Maybe you're coming
from on-prem,

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:12.980
or maybe you've had some
custom deal going on

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:15.300
with a particular company.

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:18.860
So in that case, we support
the BYOL model, which

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:20.120
is bring your own license.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.749
And in this case, you're still
provisioning virtual machines.

00:21:22.749 --> 00:21:24.290
You're doing it
through the launcher.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:27.410
So the configuration
is all done for you.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.990
You're getting a single
bill, but the bill

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.920
is only covering your
infrastructure cost.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.330
The over the top or
license fee or usage

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.417
is done separately
through the vendor.

00:21:37.417 --> 00:21:39.500
But you're still able to
get the benefits of Cloud

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.960
Launcher in sophisticated
deployments,

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.510
easy configuration, et cetera.

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:47.540
And these support, of
course, single and multi

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:49.880
VM configurations.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:52.640
So that covers
Compute Engine VM's.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.050
Next is SaaS.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:55.220
So software as a service--

