documentation, et cetera.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:47.040
One of the nice things about
GCP is our SSH support.

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.761
So we have built in
SSH just off the web.

00:32:49.761 --> 00:32:51.260
You don't need to
fire up a console.

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:52.634
You don't need to
configure keys.

00:32:52.634 --> 00:32:53.590
It's all built in.

00:32:53.590 --> 00:32:57.720
And all of our Cloud Launcher
package software VM solutions

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:00.350
have this functionality
built in as well.

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:04.120
So just as a proof to make sure
you're not being tricked here,

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:06.700
this blog is up
and running live.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:09.190
And just as a proof for
that you can see here.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:11.667
And I configured it to say this.

00:33:11.667 --> 00:33:13.750
So really just a matter
of minutes, you can get up

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:14.890
and running with a blog.

00:33:14.890 --> 00:33:16.690
Really straightforward.

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:19.660
So that's a pretty
straightforward application

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:20.470
of Cloud Launcher.

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:22.678
But now let's think about
something a little bit more

00:33:22.678 --> 00:33:23.300
sophisticated.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:27.460
So before I came to Google, I
had run a couple of startups,

00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:29.530
and we were heavy
users in MongoDB.

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:32.050
And I would tell you
that we spent, God knows,

00:33:32.050 --> 00:33:35.980
how many hours configuring
MongoDB even in the Cloud.

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:38.080
Because we had a
set of replica sets.

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:40.120
We needed multi
zone configuration,

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.440
and it was quite a challenge.

00:33:42.440 --> 00:33:45.300
So I'm going to show you
how you can deploy MongoDB

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:47.672
at scale, quite easily
through the Cloud Launcher.

00:33:47.672 --> 00:33:49.130
So first thing
we're going to do is

00:33:49.130 --> 00:33:51.050
we're going to search for Mongo.

00:33:51.050 --> 00:33:53.920
And in this case, I'm going
to choose the multi VM

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:56.560
version that we have through
Google Click to Deploy.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:58.030
Again, same details apply.

00:33:58.030 --> 00:33:59.590
In this case, it's
telling me a lot

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:02.020
about the pricing, because
we have multiple VMs

00:34:02.020 --> 00:34:03.950
in this configuration.

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:06.730
Including the sustained use
discount, which is quite hefty.

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:11.080
And so once I decide I like
this, I get to this nice UI.

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.630
And this time you see different
options for my WordPress.

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:17.739
This time I can set up exactly
the number of nodes I need.

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:20.290
So we have, again,
intelligent defaults.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:22.834
We start with a
two-node cluster.

00:34:22.834 --> 00:34:24.250
But you might want
more, depending

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:25.540
on your configuration.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:27.639
You can change the machine
type for each node.

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:29.650
We've preconfigured
it to be four

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:31.989
VCPU's and 26 Gigs of memory.

00:34:31.989 --> 00:34:34.120
You can change the
disk, and, of course,

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:36.159
you can change the
size of the disk.

00:34:36.159 --> 00:34:38.980
And if you've installed MongoDB
before, you know it's not just

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:41.409
about the nodes that
are powering MongoDB,

00:34:41.409 --> 00:34:44.920
you also need an additional node
to scale, called an arbiter.

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:46.553
But the arbiter
isn't doing much.

00:34:46.553 --> 00:34:48.219
It's just there in
case any of the nodes

00:34:48.219 --> 00:34:51.280
go down so it can do
master re-election.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.610
So we've programmed
that intelligent default

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:56.139
into the solution.

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:57.130
And you see it here.

00:34:57.130 --> 00:34:59.262
Where we have an
arbiter, and if you

00:34:59.262 --> 00:35:00.970
look at the machine
type for the arbiter,

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:03.567
it's one of the smallest
machines we have.

00:35:03.567 --> 00:35:05.650
Again, because it's not
doing a whole lot of work.

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:06.983
It doesn't need a lot of memory.

00:35:06.983 --> 00:35:08.710
Certainly doesn't
need a lot of CPU.

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:10.510
And so these are
just some of the ways

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:14.860
where we've looked at a problem,
created an architecture,

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:16.750
built intelligent
defaults, and allow

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:21.130
you to deploy with all of that
knowledge just in a few clicks.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:23.430
So I'll go ahead
and click Deploy.

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:29.006
Similar to last time I've
done this before this talk.

00:35:29.006 --> 00:35:30.880
So I'll show you what
that post-deploy screen

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:33.090
looks like for MongoDB here.

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:35.200
It's this one here.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:37.300
Again, similar to our
WordPress example,

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:39.230
but some different
options this time.

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:39.730
Right?

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:41.271
Here we're talking
about, how you can

00:35:41.271 --> 00:35:43.310
connect to the MongoDB primary?

00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:46.180
Link you to documentation
and support.

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:48.460
So we've seen simple
configurations,

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:49.210
like a WordPress.

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:52.270
We've seen a multi VM
configuration, like Mongo.

00:35:52.270 --> 00:35:54.729
But let's say you're
using this MongoDB,

00:35:54.729 --> 00:35:56.770
and it's a couple of months
after you deployed it

00:35:56.770 --> 00:35:57.699
from the Launcher.

00:35:57.699 --> 00:35:58.490
And you say, great.

00:35:58.490 --> 00:35:59.365
This is working well.

00:35:59.365 --> 00:36:00.970
I've deployed it in one zone.

00:36:00.970 --> 00:36:03.170
I now need to deploy
in yet another zone.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:04.271
So how would you do that?

00:36:04.271 --> 00:36:05.770
Well, now what I'm
going to show you

00:36:05.770 --> 00:36:09.280
is how you can use Deployment
Manager to actually redeploy

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:11.650
the same Mongo solution
into a different zone,

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:13.420
with maybe different settings.

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:15.530
So what I've done is,
in this deployment,

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:18.910
I've copied over the
Deployment Manager scripts.

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:20.580
And they're all listed here.

00:36:20.580 --> 00:36:22.360
I'm just going to
show you my terminal.

00:36:22.360 --> 00:36:23.740
So I've downloaded them here.

00:36:23.740 --> 00:36:25.660
And it's just a few files.

00:36:25.660 --> 00:36:30.120
And I'm going to open up
the configuration file.

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:31.500
And we're going to do this live.

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:34.060
And so this is a
configuration file that's

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:36.130
Deployment Manager Template.

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:38.650
And the part I want you
to focus on is down here.

00:36:38.650 --> 00:36:40.780
And these are basically
parameters that we

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:44.890
can set to configure
this Mongo solution.

00:36:44.890 --> 00:36:46.470
Now if you're paying
attention, you

00:36:46.470 --> 00:36:48.730
might realize that a
lot of these parameters

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:50.782
are the ones that you saw
in our user experience.

00:36:50.782 --> 00:36:52.240
And that's exactly
what's going on.

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:54.489
Our user experience is really
driving these Deployment

00:36:54.489 --> 00:36:56.450
Manager templates
behind the scenes.

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:58.210
And so now, we're
going under the hood.

00:36:58.210 --> 00:36:59.470
And we're actually opening
up that Deployment Manager

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:00.670
Template.

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:05.890
And instead of deploying
this MongoDB in the zone US

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:09.370
central one F, which is what
we had before, we are actually

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:11.870
can deploy it in US Central 1A.

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:13.450
I cam change other parameters.

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:15.010
For example, the
number of replicas

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:16.700
or the size of a machine.

00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:20.410
But in this case, I'm happy
with just changing the zone.

00:37:20.410 --> 00:37:22.150
So I'm going to hit Save.

00:37:22.150 --> 00:37:25.000
And what I'm going to do now
is jump to the command line.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:32.740
And using the G Cloud command
line for deploy manager, all

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:34.210
I'm doing is just
specifying this

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:36.190
file-- this MongoDB
dot Yaml-- and I'm

00:37:36.190 --> 00:37:39.490
telling G Cloud deploy manager,
create a new deployment.

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.410
Call it Mongo Prod Next.

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:43.951
And here's a Config file.

00:37:43.951 --> 00:37:45.700
So I'm going to go
ahead and fire that up.

00:37:45.700 --> 00:37:48.620
And what this is going to
do is go behind the scenes,

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:52.090
go into our data center, bring
up a cluster of three nodes,

00:37:52.090 --> 00:37:54.920
set up MongoDB, configure
all the SSH keys.

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:57.522
Making sure that each node
understands the IP addresses

00:37:57.522 --> 00:37:58.480
of all the other nodes.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:37:59.350
Set up the arbiter.

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:00.849
All that stuff which
would typically

00:38:00.849 --> 00:38:03.250
take you several hours
or sometimes even days.

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:05.320
If you get it right
the first time.

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.270
And it's doing this
all behind the scenes.

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:09.940
And it's going to deploy
now in a different zone.

00:38:09.940 --> 00:38:13.570
And so this might take me
another 30 seconds or a minute

00:38:13.570 --> 00:38:14.480
to finish up.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:18.800
But, in the meantime, we'll
go back to our UI here.

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:19.300
Here we go.

00:38:19.300 --> 00:38:20.650
Actually it's done.

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:21.310
So here we go.

00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:22.780
It's the deploy
manager telling us

00:38:22.780 --> 00:38:25.780
that it's done
running its script.

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:28.850
And those instances are now up
and running, and we can use it.

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:31.800
So, as you can see, the
power of Deployment Manager

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:35.200
is you can embed this directly
in your CICD pipeline.

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:36.820
It's not just for
kicking the tires.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:40.310
You can actually run these
solutions in production.

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:41.792
So let's wrap up
some of this demo.

00:38:41.792 --> 00:38:43.750
Other couple of points
I want to tell you about

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:46.870
is, sometimes these solutions
take a while to deploy.

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:48.400
And you might be impatient.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:50.500
If you're like me, want
to get other things done.

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:51.710
So we've done a few things.

00:38:51.710 --> 00:38:54.190
One is whenever you deploy
one of these solutions,

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.710
we actually send you
an email notification.

00:38:56.710 --> 00:38:59.770
And we'll let you know that
your deployment is ready.

00:38:59.770 --> 00:39:01.150
And it's not just
a notification,

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:03.760
we actually again connect
you to documentation.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:05.765
Our goal here is to
make sure that whenever

00:39:05.765 --> 00:39:08.140
you choose a piece of software,
and you want to get it up

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:10.360
and running, you have
everything you need

00:39:10.360 --> 00:39:12.220
to be successful in doing so.

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:15.430
And this is just
another example of that.

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:17.200
Last but not least,
I want to show you

00:39:17.200 --> 00:39:21.050
what it looks like to
purchase SaaS solutions.

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:23.020
So let's say you are
building your application.

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:24.561
You've got your
Mongo up and running.

00:39:24.561 --> 00:39:25.720
You've got your app server.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:29.590
And now you need to send
e-mails to your customers.

00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:31.420
So might come in here.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.680
You might use our
global search bar.

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:35.410
You might type an email.

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:36.700
And you see Sun Grid.

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:39.550
And so, this time, we're not
deploying any virtual machines.

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:41.380
This is a SaaS offering.

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:43.410
But this is going
to have a billing

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:44.410
integration with Google.

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:45.580
So you just come down here.

00:39:45.580 --> 00:39:48.350
You get to see all the
different plans they have.

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:51.610
And once you are happy with
it, you can click Subscribe.

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:54.520
And this shows up again
on your Google bill.

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:57.130
And beyond that, we
have all the tutorials,

00:39:57.130 --> 00:40:00.130
references for the API's in
different languages all built

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:01.230
in.

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:04.570
And you can be up and
running in no time.

00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:07.365
Let's switch back to the slides.

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:12.990
So just to recap, between
Orbitera Test Drives and Cloud

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:13.490
Launcher.

00:40:13.490 --> 00:40:16.979
Orbitera Test Drives-- great
way to try solutions at no cost.

00:40:16.979 --> 00:40:19.020
You don't have to worry
about free trial credits.

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:23.440
You can work directly with
the ISV to learn more.

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:27.420
Cloud Launcher is a great
way to get started on GCP.

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:28.809
Also run things in production.

00:40:28.809 --> 00:40:31.350
We're really trying to make it
our one-stop shop for products

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:33.240
and services in GCP.

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:36.120
We've added hundreds
of solutions

00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:37.540
over the last couple of years.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.200
And we're adding
them at a record pace

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:41.210
actually, going forward.

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:43.230
So, highly suggest
you check it out

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:44.490
if you haven't seen it before.

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:46.290
Or if you're are
an existing user,

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:49.040
take a look at all the
inventory we've added.

